[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports describe
three problems. The parser fix fixed only the first one. Each problem now has a ticket of its
own.

## Where each problem stands

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October.

- `parser`: **fixed** by "fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales".
  [#1](https://github.com/kevmiota/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/1) is closed as completed.
- `hint`: **won't fix**, because the booking-v3 form is retired.
  [#2](https://github.com/kevmiota/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/2) is closed as not planned.
- `move`: **still open.** The engineering update on #4471 does not cover it, and the parser fix
  does not touch it. [#3](https://github.com/kevmiota/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/3) stays open.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day move is not fixed. A new report of a booking that moves by one
day belongs in #3, not in a reopened #4471.
</Callout>

## The problems

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table counts by tenant and channel, the desk's own view. The reports it footnotes as a
one-day move are counted inside `parser` there, and they are the third problem below.

- **`parser`** ([#1](https://github.com/kevmiota/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/1)): the shared parser
  reads every date month first. Our tenants write the day first, so the day and the month
  swap: "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July."
- **`hint`** ([#2](https://github.com/kevmiota/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/2)): the booking-v3 form
  prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants type what the hint
  says, and the form reads it day first. The result is also a swap, so these reports look like
  `parser`, but the parser fix does not touch them. Only reports sent through the form of a
  booking-v3 tenant have this cause. The table marks this row "no ticket of its own"; its
  ticket is #2.
- **one-day move** ([#3](https://github.com/kevmiota/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/3)): the date moves
  by one day, or a meeting lands on two different days for two time zones: "Client in Lisbon
  got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." No day and month swap here. These are the
  reports in `desk-notes.json`, footnoted under the table.

## How the reports were placed

- Each report is counted once (`uniqueById`). The export pages overlap, so some reports
  appear twice in the file.
- `parser` and `hint` come from the tenant and the channel (`causeOf` in `causes.ts`), not from
  the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser and disagrees with the tenant on
  many reports.

## Why it was merged as one

The [dedupe rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) merges on the subject line. All three problems produce
the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so subject matching put them in
one ticket. [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) accepted that reports with
different subjects about one bug would split. It did not consider the opposite case: reports
with one subject about different bugs. That case is what happened here.

